Yes, exactly. Terraform can create some resources successfully and then fail on a later resource.

Example:
terraform apply
      ↓
VPC      → created ✅
Subnet   → created ✅
EC2      → ERROR ❌

The VPC and subnet will normally remain created. Terraform does not automatically delete everything just because one resource failed.
Terraform also records the successfully created resources in the state:
terraform.tfstate
    ↓
VPC ✅
Subnet ✅
EC2 ❌

Then you look at the error, fix the problem, and run:
terraform apply

Terraform checks the state and sees:

VPC      → already exists ✅ → skip
Subnet   → already exists ✅ → skip
EC2      → not created ❌ → create

So it continues from the missing/incorrect part, rather than normally creating another VPC and subnet.

